From 4b3e540176afa3c3c568a00a7a64f518a7d7dfb2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: stew675 <stew675@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 27 Nov 2026 07:29:39 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] wip: flash-next-prefill, the whole campaign as ONE change
 (squashed, reviewed, rename to PIPELINE)

The campaign's 21 commits, squashed because the code churned a lot across five sessions and the diff is the only
reviewable form.  Nothing in the delivery changes: this is the prototype tree on the r368 block set (base
919794500, block tip af7a57ba8).

What it does, in one paragraph: it replaces the slot-ring staging transport with a one-to-three-buffer PIPELINE
(the maintainer's design) whose buffers live in the slab's transport region, makes that transport the default at
prefill width behind a measured selector (host bytes the gather would pull per token vs 38 us), and fixes the
class of defects that only a real server's SECOND prefill reaches (the bump cursor was never returned, the carve
was never released, a decode/verify compute-band raise was deferred for ever).  The transport region is sized
from one split's host-weight set, is re-carved per wide pass, and sits on a cushion of N compute chunks above the
decode/verify band so that band can grow without moving it -- and its DEPTH is chosen from the expert-cache
residency once the arena settles, not from free VRAM at startup.  Around it: the hybrid/bulk fill for a tensor
split, the merge-floor calibration to the device link, the arena settle re-opening when capacity grows, and the
D1 probes kept default-off with their measurements.

Reviewed as a squashed diff (wip/flash-next-prefill/REVIEW-squashed.md) with every finding fixed in this commit:
two format-string bugs (a dropped argument in the transport-decision line, a %zu fed an int in the
transport-exhaustion WARN), a Meta iface entry that was default-initialised instead of declared, the dead
free-VRAM start-up sizing and the ring-era per-split width sizing, a stale Meta slot default, a duplicated
forward-declaration comment, and five comments/log lines that described the deleted decision or predicted a number
the code did not set.  One behavioural fix: the pipeline collects a split's stageable set BEFORE it touches the
width gate, because that gate runs the one-off H2D calibration (a cudaMalloc probe Windows does not get back,
issue #97).

The review's four judgement calls, decided by the maintainer and implemented:

  J1  the buffer-count override is clamped to the PIPELINE's own 1..3.
  J2  the per-graph split list is 512 entries (was 256) and says so once when it fills (measured ceilings here:
      Flash-Next 144, 122B-A10B 120).  Past the cap those splits take the serial upload -- the path this
      transport exists to replace -- and the old code was silent about it.
  J3  the residency mirrors are O(1): `covered` IS the per-device resident figure `arena_bytes_delta` already
      keeps, and the host total only changes when a table is registered.  Measured the old scan before removing
      it: 0.3 us per call, 146 us by the first drop and 197 us across the whole three-request sequence, against
      ~170-990 ms drop pauses (so a hash map was never warranted).  Because that scan doubled as a SAFETY NET -- it
      recomputed `covered` from `t.slots` -- two things came with the change: the one path that clears `t.slots`
      without the accounting (`moe_cache_shrink_arena`, caller-less today, kept for a future pressure release) now
      calls `arena_bytes_delta`, and the exit census re-derives both mirrors from the table set and warns on drift
      (`residency_audit_locked`), so the invariant is checkable instead of trusted.
  J4  `GGML_SCHED_PINGPONG_BUFS` -> `GGML_SCHED_PIPELINE_BUFS` and `GGML_SCHED_PINGPONG` ->
      `GGML_SCHED_PIPELINE` (the old spellings plus the deleted ring's `GGML_SCHED_STAGE_SLOTS` are deprecated
      aliases, read in ONE place -- the CUDA backend's authority, which the scheduler and Meta read through the
      existing `slab_ring_slots` hook); every "ping-pong" in this campaign's code, logs and identifiers is now
      "pipeline", and the three unrelated upstream uses are untouched.  The kill switch's comment now says what it
      does (no staged transport at all -- the ring it used to select is gone).

Two deadlocks of the same class were hit and fixed in this campaign, both "a function that takes g_mutex called
from a context that already holds it": `moe_cache_host_pool_requested()` from inside `alloc_all_locked` (the first
version of the residency rule re-requested the region through `slab_ring_region_bytes`), and an audit that took
`g_mutex` from inside `report_locked()`.  Both are named at their call sites now, and both were caught by the
server hanging -- the second one after answering every request, which is why the `_locked` convention is stated in
the comment.

Verified: 1 GPU 89k greedy text 43d0b1f4629e on every run (2456-2485 t/s); the field three-request sequence
reproduces to the MiB (decision `3 -> 2`, region 3412.5 -> 2275.0 MiB, carves 2304, arena after each drop
15 960.2 / 15 960.2 / 15 827.7 MiB, prefill 2224.6 t/s, selector `exposed 30513.2 MiB / 8192 tok at 28.6 GB/s =
136.3 us/tok vs 38.0 -> STAGE (cache band 16 tok)`), the exit census reaches its summary and the mirror audit
reports no drift.
